      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, there were arguments and criticisms leveled against labor unions during that time, focusing primarily on potential negative economic and political impacts. Supporters of employers and free-market proponents argued that unions, through strikes and other collective actions, could disrupt production and adversely affect the economy by decreasing overall productivity. Critics also expressed concern that unions concentrated too much power in their hands, potentially enabling corrupt or ineffective leadership and stifling individual autonomy. Equally, some argued that the political influence wielded by unions, such as the support of particular political parties, could lead to polarization and hinder bipartisan cooperation. Despite these criticisms, the overall perception of unions in their early 20th-century heyday was overwhelmingly positive, viewing them as critical institutions for balancing power between workers and employers.",
